branding/ntp-branding.patch
branding/profile-lockdown.patch
# The Android phone/tablet workspace drawn behind the real WebContents, with the renderer's
# emulation scale kept locked to the aperture through startup, resize and whole-device zoom.
# LINUX AND WINDOWS. It was Linux-only until 2026-08-26, which is why an Android profile opened
# flush-left with no phone stage on Windows: lobium_device_frame_view.cc is unconditionally in the
# chrome ui target and compiled fine there, but every CALL SITE in browser_view.cc was
# #if BUILDFLAG(IS_LINUX), so nothing referenced the class, the linker dropped the whole object, and
# even its two switch strings were absent from chrome.dll. The view is 406 lines of portable
# views/gfx code with no platform includes, so the fix was the guards, not the implementation. Cut
# against pristine browser_view.cc, so it comes AFTER branding/profile-window-icon.patch (which
# hooks the same file) and after core/device-emulation-scale.patch, whose
# content::SetLobiumDeviceEmulationScale it calls.
branding/device-frame.patch

# Present on disk but deliberately NOT in the series:
#   branding/suppress-sandbox-infobar.patch - only needed for --no-sandbox dev runs.
#
# branding/device-frame.patch was OUT of the series from 2026-08-21 to 2026-08-25 because it did not
# link on Linux, then the only platform it targeted: its hooks compile there and reference
# LobiumDeviceFrameView, whose implementation was in no GN target, so `chrome` failed at the final
# link with seven undefined symbols (56069 of 56070 steps had succeeded, on 152.0.7977.42). It is
# back because the two pieces it was missing are now in the series instead of being untracked files
# on a single build host: core/build-gn.patch adds views/frame/lobium_device_frame_view.{cc,h} to
# the chrome ui target, and core/device-emulation-scale.patch carries
# content/{public/browser,browser/devtools}/lobium_device_emulation.* together with the
# EmulationHandler::SetDeviceEmulationScale it calls - a method that until then existed in no file
# at all, which is why the feature could never have linked however it was wired.
# See ../hooks.md "device frame".
